     # one run this project had to discard, because a model wrote a falsifier
     # that opened the scoring key. This does not touch that.
     #
-    # A model-authored falsifier cannot reach this: the value is read by the
-    # PARENT process that scans the source, and the falsifier runs as a child.
+    # WHAT KEEPS THIS SAFE IS NOT WHAT THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY (2026-08-31).
+    #
+    # It read: "A model-authored falsifier cannot reach this: the value is read
+    # by the PARENT process that scans the source, and the falsifier runs as a
+    # child." The stated reason is FALSE, and false about this very function.
+    # `_allow_roots` has three callers, and only one of them is the parent-side
+    # source scan. At :642 `_observer_source` calls it to build the allowlist it
+    # BAKES INTO THE OBSERVER, which is then installed via PYTHONPATH and runs
+    # INSIDE the child. Measured 2026-08-31 by rendering the observer with
+    # CDSFL_CANONICAL_REPO set: the directory appears in the child's runtime
+    # `allow` list. The value reaches the child on every run.
+    #
+    # A wrong reason sitting beside a permission boundary is worse than no
+    # reason, because the next person to widen this reads it as clearance. This
+    # boundary is the one closed after the single run this project had to
+    # discard, when a model wrote a falsifier that opened the scoring key.
+    #
+    # THE TWO CLAIMS THAT ARE ACTUALLY TRUE, and they are different claims:
+    #
+    #   1. A falsifier cannot CONTROL the value. It is read from the parent's
+    #      environment, only the panel dispatcher sets it, and the child's own
+    #      environment is scrubbed of it by `_sandbox_env` -- so nothing the
+    #      model writes can add a root here.
+    #   2. Being allowed is NOT being able to read the key. The observer's
+    #      `_denied` tests the protected set FIRST and refuses a protected
+    #      location even inside an allowed root -- "the allowlist is a
+    #      convenience, the protected set is the point". The scoring key is
+    #      carried as SHA-256 digests, so widening a root does not widen access
+    #      to it.
+    #
+    # So the widening is real and intended, and what bounds it is the protected
+    # set one layer further in -- not the parent/child split named above.
     _canon = os.environ.get("CDSFL_CANONICAL_REPO", "").strip()
     if _canon and os.path.isdir(_canon):
         roots.append(os.path.normpath(_canon))
